e:T490,The reading-listening gap is rooted in how orthography affects language processing. <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/applied-psycholinguistics/article/second-language-phonology-at-the-interface-between-acoustic-and-orthographic-input/349B5CD70A06209C334EB78454305D25" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bassetti, Escudero &amp; Hayes-Harb (2015)</a> reviewed research showing that orthographic input interacts with phonological development in complex ways — while text can help learners form distinct word representations, it can also prevent them from developing accurate auditory perception of sound distinctions. Learners who process language primarily through text may develop strong visual-phonological mappings that don't fully engage acoustic processing, which explains why removing subtitles feels so disorienting. The good news, supported by <a href="https://doi.org/10.3138/cmlr.63.1.59" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nation (2006)</a> on vocabulary knowledge and listening, is that you already have the linguistic knowledge; Phase 3 retrains your perceptual system to access what you've learned without text as a crutch.1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"phase-3","kind":"phase-index","route":"/roadmap/phase-3","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/phase-3","sourceHash":"e0121ba3493025dc3b2110b386669be8970770536fbc2704cc510fa1f416be07","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T23:11:46.466Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"Listening","zh":"听力"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"You've spent Phase 2 building strong reading comprehension — you can follow native content with subtitles and understand most of what you read. But if someone turned off the subtitles right now, you'd probably feel lost.","zh":"你在第2阶段培养了扎实的阅读理解能力——借助字幕，你可以跟上母语内容，并理解所读内容的大部分。但如果现在有人关掉字幕，你可能会立刻感到不知所措。"}},{"id":"segment-0002","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"That gap between your reading and your listening is completely normal, and closing it is the entire focus of Phase 3. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/the-reading-listening-gap\">The Reading-Listening Gap</a>","zh":"阅读和听力之间存在差距完全正常，而弥合这一差距正是第3阶段的全部重点。<a href=\"/roadmap/library/the-reading-listening-gap\">阅读—听力差距</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The good news is that you already know the words, the grammar, and the patterns. Your brain has all the raw material it needs. The problem is that your ears haven't had enough practice decoding those things at natural speed, without text as a crutch. Once you start training your listening directly, everything clicks into place much faster than you'd expect.","zh":"好消息是，你已经认识这些词，掌握了语法和语言模式。你的大脑拥有所需的一切原材料。问题在于，你的耳朵还没有足够练习，无法在没有文字辅助的情况下，以自然语速解码这些内容。一旦你开始直接训练听力，一切会比你预想中更快地融会贯通。"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Phase 3 is also where you'll start preparing for real conversations — first by training your ears on conversational speech, then by practicing the comprehension side of conversation through crosstalk. Some light pronunciation and speaking practice also begins here as a secondary activity. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/crosstalk\">Crosstalk</a>","zh":"第3阶段也是你开始为真实对话做准备的阶段——先训练耳朵适应会话语音，再通过交叉对话练习对话中的理解部分。这里也会开始一些轻量的发音和口语练习，作为次要活动。<a href=\"/roadmap/library/crosstalk\">交叉对话</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0005","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"By the end of Phase 3, you'll understand native speakers in real-time conversation and be ready to start speaking yourself.","zh":"到第3阶段结束时，你将能够理解母语者的实时对话，并准备好开始自己说话。"}},{"id":"hour-estimates","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Hour Estimates","zh":"时间估算"}},{"id":"segment-0007","type":"table","rows":[[{"en":"","zh":"","header":true},{"en":"Cousin","zh":"近亲","header":true},{"en":"Similar","zh":"相近","header":true},{"en":"Neutral","zh":"中性","header":true},{"en":"Distant","zh":"远亲","header":true}],[{"en":"This phase","zh":"本阶段","header":false},{"en":"220","zh":"220","header":false},{"en":"300","zh":"300","header":false},{"en":"400","zh":"400","header":false},{"en":"750","zh":"750","header":false}],[{"en":"Cumulative","zh":"累计","header":false},{"en":"440","zh":"440","header":false},{"en":"750","zh":"750","header":false},{"en":"900","zh":"900","header":false},{"en":"1500","zh":"1500","header":false}]]},{"id":"sub-phases","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Sub-phases","zh":"子阶段"}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>3A — Overcome the Listening Gap:</strong> Face the initial shock of losing your subtitles and build core listening ability through intensive listening exercises.","zh":"<strong>3A——克服听读差距：</strong>面对失去字幕辅助时最初的不适，并通过强化听力练习培养核心听力能力。"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>3B — Choosing a Dialect:</strong> Pick a specific dialect and accent to focus on, and tune your ears to how it sounds.","zh":"<strong>3B——选择方言：</strong>选择一种特定的方言和口音作为重点，并让耳朵适应它的声音特点。"}},{"id":"segment-0011","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>3C — Core Listening Skills:</strong> Strengthen your ears through focused exercises like transcription and listen looping until you can follow TV without subtitles.","zh":"<strong>3C——核心听力技能：</strong>通过听写和循环听音等专注练习强化听力，直到你能在没有字幕的情况下看懂电视节目。"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>3D — Understand Native Conversation:</strong> Shift your focus to conversational speech and practice the comprehension side of conversation through crosstalk.","zh":"<strong>3D——理解母语者对话：</strong>将重点转向会话语音，并通过交叉对话练习对话中的理解能力。"}},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与依据"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"$e","zh":"阅读—听力差距根源于正字法对语言加工的影响。<a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/applied-psycholinguistics/article/second-language-phonology-at-the-interface-between-acoustic-and-orthographic-input/349B5CD70A06209C334EB78454305D25\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Bassetti、Escudero 和 Hayes-Harb（2015）</a>回顾了相关研究，指出正字法输入会以复杂的方式与音系发展相互作用：文字可以帮助学习者建立清晰的词语表征，但也可能妨碍他们发展对声音差异的准确听觉感知。主要通过文字加工语言的学习者，可能会形成强大的视觉—音系映射，却没有充分调动听觉加工，这解释了为什么去掉字幕会让人如此不适应。好消息是，<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3138/cmlr.63.1.59\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nation（2006）</a>关于词汇知识和听力的研究支持这样一点：你已经具备语言知识；第3阶段要做的是重新训练你的感知系统，让你在没有文字辅助的情况下调用所学内容。"}},{"id":"segment-0015","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Crosstalk is a practical application of <a href=\"https://www.sdkrashen.com/content/books/principles_and_practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Krashen's (1982)</a> core principles: it provides interactive, personalized comprehensible input while keeping the affective filter low by removing the pressure to produce in the target language. The conversational context means the input is naturally adjusted, contextually rich, and personally relevant — all factors that promote acquisition — without the cognitive load and anxiety that come from trying to speak before you're ready.","zh":"交叉对话是对<a href=\"https://www.sdkrashen.com/content/books/principles_and_practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Krashen（1982）</a>核心原则的实际应用：它提供互动、个性化且可理解的输入，同时通过消除用目标语言输出的压力来降低情感过滤。对话语境意味着输入会自然地得到调整，内容丰富且与个人相关——这些因素都能促进习得——同时避免了尚未准备好就尝试说话所带来的认知负担和焦虑。"}}],"cards":[{"href":"/roadmap/phase-3/3a-overcome-the-listening-gap","title":{"en":"3AOvercome the Listening Gap","zh":"3A克服听读差距"}},{"href":"/roadmap/phase-3/3b-choosing-a-dialect","title":{"en":"3BChoosing a Dialect","zh":"3B选择方言"}},{"href":"/roadmap/phase-3/3c-core-listening-skills","title":{"en":"3CCore Listening Skills","zh":"3C核心听力技能"}},{"href":"/roadmap/phase-3/3d-understand-native-conversation","title":{"en":"3DUnderstand Native Conversation","zh":"3D理解母语者对话"}}]},"siteBasePath":"/my_learning_docs"}]
